\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Selection function of the searched sample against a volume-limited reference census: Gaia\nobreakspace  {}DR3 sources with $\varpi >0$, $\sigma _\varpi /\varpi \le 0.1$ and $1/\varpi \le 40$\,pc (17566 objects, of which 8594 carry a \texttt  {teff\_gspphot} estimate, 49 per cent), against the 89 stars with at least one searched window. Temperatures are available for 53 of the 89 (all 89 matched to the 168-entry ALMA-covered census, 56 by exact name; 53 of those matches carry a temperature), so the temperature panel is normalised over classified objects only. ``Ratio'' is the sample column fraction over the census column fraction; $>1$ is over-representation.}}{4}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:selfunc}{{1}{4}{Selection function of the searched sample against a volume-limited reference census: Gaia~DR3 sources with $\varpi >0$, $\sigma _\varpi /\varpi \le 0.1$ and $1/\varpi \le 40$\,pc (17566 objects, of which 8594 carry a \texttt {teff\_gspphot} estimate, 49 per cent), against the 89 stars with at least one searched window. Temperatures are available for 53 of the 89 (all 89 matched to the 168-entry ALMA-covered census, 56 by exact name; 53 of those matches carry a temperature), so the temperature panel is normalised over classified objects only. ``Ratio'' is the sample column fraction over the census column fraction; $>1$ is over-representation}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The selection funnel, from the catalogued population within 40\,pc to the stars with a searched window. The three quantities the text keeps apart are the 168-entry work list the archive query returns, the 115{} stars a public field genuinely contains, and the 89{} that yielded a searchable window. The chain applied to what comes out of the funnel is Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:chain}.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:funnel}{{2}{4}{The selection funnel, from the catalogued population within 40\,pc to the stars with a searched window. The three quantities the text keeps apart are the \NCensus -entry work list the archive query returns, the \NGenuinelyCovered {} stars a public field genuinely contains, and the \NStars {} that yielded a searchable window. The chain applied to what comes out of the funnel is Fig.~\ref {fig:chain}}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Systems of particular interest: every sample system within 15\,pc hosting at least one confirmed planet, ordered by distance. $N_{\rm  pl}$ is the confirmed-planet count, $N_{\rm  A}$ the number of Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows, EIRP$_{90}$ the deepest Class\nobreakspace  {}A sensitivity reached, and $N_{\rm  eb}$ the number of execution blocks covering the system, so $N_{\rm  eb}>1$ means repeat epochs exist. Six of these systems have no Class\nobreakspace  {}A coverage and so carry no drift-resolving limit --- the archival selection of \S  \ref  {sec:bias} acting on exactly the targets a transmitter search would choose. The full target list is in the data release.}}{4}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:interesting}{{2}{4}{Systems of particular interest: every sample system within 15\,pc hosting at least one confirmed planet, ordered by distance. $N_{\rm pl}$ is the confirmed-planet count, $N_{\rm A}$ the number of Class~A windows, EIRP$_{90}$ the deepest Class~A sensitivity reached, and $N_{\rm eb}$ the number of execution blocks covering the system, so $N_{\rm eb}>1$ means repeat epochs exist. Six of these systems have no Class~A coverage and so carry no drift-resolving limit --- the archival selection of \S \ref {sec:bias} acting on exactly the targets a transmitter search would choose. The full target list is in the data release}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The survey as searched. Every frequency union quoted here is an aggregate over clustered, repeatedly revisited coverage, whose distribution is Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:waterfall}; no union should be read as continuous coverage between its endpoints. Powers and frequencies are given to two significant figures, except where a channel or sky frequency must be matched to the released catalogue and is given to the precision the grid supports.}}{4}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:searchspace}{{3}{4}{The survey as searched. Every frequency union quoted here is an aggregate over clustered, repeatedly revisited coverage, whose distribution is Fig.~\ref {fig:waterfall}; no union should be read as continuous coverage between its endpoints. Powers and frequencies are given to two significant figures, except where a channel or sky frequency must be matched to the released catalogue and is given to the precision the grid supports}{table.3}{}}
